{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Split computed but ignored: loop uses raw glob",
    "Explanation": [
      "The config sets parallelism: 2 on the test job. The split command does run and produces a correct per-node assignment (node 0 gets src/test_goodbye.sh, node 1 gets src/test_hello.sh), but the output is written to /tmp/split.txt and never used to drive execution. The loop iterates over the shell glob `src/test_*.sh`, so each node executes both files — the log shows both 'Running src/test_goodbye.sh' and 'Running src/test_hello.sh' on node 0 and node 1. Thus the suite was not actually split (each node ran the full suite twice over the two nodes), and the built-in guard (RAN=2 vs EXPECTED=1) trips, exiting 1 on both nodes. The fix is not to change parallelism or the workflow; it is to actually consume the `circleci tests split` output — i.e., loop over the files listed in /tmp/split.txt (the split list) rather than the raw glob. That makes each node run only its assigned file, covering the suite exactly once and satisfying the RAN==EXPECTED check so the job goes green."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
      "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
      "  \"suite_split\": false",
      "}"
    ],
    "ExpectedAnswer": [
      [
        "{",
        "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
        "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
        "  \"suite_split\": false",
        "}"
      ]
    ],
